        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "0cdf41a6-bfa3-5ab7-81be-4823a1b4d1fc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Companies: Memotech, Talking Electronics, Level 9 Computing\n- Products: Memotech MTX500, MTX512, RS128, TEC-1\n- People: Mike Austin, Nicholas Austin, Pete Austin\n- Platforms: BBC Micro, Nascom, ZX Spectrum, Commodore 64, Oric, Atari, Lynx 48k, RML 380Z, Amstrad CPC, MSX, Amiga, Apple II, Memotech MTX, Enterprise\n- Concepts: home computers, single-board kit computers, text adventure games\n\nFacts:\n- Talking Electronics first produced the TEC-1, a single-board kit computer, in the early 1980s.\n- Memotech released the Zilog Z80A-based home computers MTX500, MTX512, and RS128 in 1983 and 1984.\n- Mike Austin, Nicholas Austin, and Pete Austin founded Level 9 Computing.\n- Level 9 Computing produced software for BBC Micro, Nascom, ZX Spectrum, Commodore 64, Oric, Atari, Lynx 48k, RML 380Z, Amstrad CPC, MSX, Amiga, Apple II, Memotech MTX, and Enterprise platforms.\n- Level 9 Computing was best known for its successful text adventure games.\n- Level 9 Computing closed in June 1991 after a general decline in the text adventure market.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6ddfea0f-21a3-5e4d-9f4d-dd6b775820b3",
        "text": "Page: Memotech MTX. Sentence: The Memotech MTX500, MTX512 and RS128 were a series of Zilog Z80A processor-based home computers released by Memotech in 1983 and 1984.\nPage: TEC-1. Sentence: The TEC-1 is a single-board kit computer first produced by the Australian hobbyist electronics magazine Talking Electronics in the early 1980s.\nPage: Level 9 Computing. Sentence: Founded by Mike, Nicholas and Pete Austin, the company produced software for the BBC Micro, Nascom, ZX Spectrum, Commodore 64, Oric, Atari, Lynx 48k, RML 380Z, Amstrad CPC, MSX, Amiga, Apple II, Memotech MTX, and Enterprise platforms and is best known for its successful text adventure games until a general decline in the text adventure market forced their closure in June 1991.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
